# 🧲 Metal Box Pi Zero 2 W Server (Headless Cyber Node)

## 🧠 Core concept

A fully headless mini server built around a  
Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W  
mounted inside a metal enclosure, running Linux server tools with no GUI.

---

## 🧱 Hardware layout

### 🧠 Brain
- Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W  
- microSD card (OS + storage)

### 🌐 Networking (recommended)
- USB OTG adapter  
- USB → Ethernet adapter (RTL8152/8153 chipset preferred)

### 🔌 Power
- Stable 5V 2.5A+ supply OR power bank  
- short, high-quality USB cable  

### 🌡 Cooling system (IMPORTANT)
- Metal box lid acts as passive heatsink  
- Thermal pad between CPU and lid  
- Optional standoffs to maintain pressure + insulation  

---

## 🧲 Metal enclosure design (heatsink concept)

### Heat flow design:
```
Pi CPU → thermal pad → metal lid → air (outside)
```

### Requirements:
- Pi must NOT touch metal directly (insulation required)
- Use thermal pad for heat transfer
- Ensure firm but safe pressure contact
- Metal box acts as a full chassis heatsink

---

## 💻 Software stack

### OS
- Raspberry Pi OS Lite OR Ubuntu Server (headless)

### Core services
- SSH (primary access method)
- optional static IP (recommended)
- optional lightweight web server (control panel)

### Useful tools
- nmap (network scanning)
- python3 (automation + robot control)
- tmux (persistent sessions)
- git (project management)
- htop (monitoring)

---

## ⚙️ System behavior

- No GUI  
- No monitor required  
- Boot → immediately available over SSH  
- Runs as always-on “command node”

---

## 🔗 Role in your ecosystem

- 🧲 Metal box = server brain (headless compute node)  
- 🍬 Mint tin = SSH macro controller / command input device  
- 🤖 Robot (DarkPaw) = physical output system  
- 📱 Laptop/phone = remote access interface  

---

# 🌡 Metal Lid Heatsink System (Passive Cooling Design)

## 🧠 Concept

Use the metal lid of the enclosure as a large passive heatsink for the Pi Zero 2 W.

---

## 🔥 How it works

- CPU generates heat  
- Thermal pad transfers heat upward  
- Metal lid spreads heat across entire surface  
- Heat dissipates into surrounding air  

---

## 🧩 Required materials

- Thermal pad (preferred) or thermal paste  
- Insulating standoffs (plastic or nylon)  
- Mounting system to apply gentle pressure  

---

## ⚙️ Construction steps

### 1. Position Pi
- Mount Pi close to the lid inside enclosure  

### 2. Add thermal interface
- Place thermal pad on CPU area  
- Ensure full contact with metal lid  

### 3. Secure alignment
- Use spacers so Pi is not crushed  
- Maintain consistent pressure contact  

---

## ⚠️ Safety rules

- Never let bare PCB touch metal  
- Avoid over-tight mounting pressure  
- Ensure SD card and GPIO pins are not stressed  
- Electrically isolate all components from enclosure  

---

## 🌡 Resulting thermal system

```
CPU → thermal pad → metal lid → ambient air
```

This turns the entire box into a passive cooling shell, similar to industrial embedded systems.
